      "title": "Govt asks Microsoft to set up data centres closer to power generation sites",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/govt-asks-microsoft-to-set-up-data-centres-closer-to-power-generation-sites-126092100649_1.html",
      "description": "The government on Monday asked Microsoft to locate future data centres closer to power generation sources rather than near cable landing stations, with Electronics and IT Secretary S Krishnan saying this would cut costs for both the company and the country.\nSpeaking at the launch of Microsoft's fourth India cloud region in Hyderabad, Krishnan said it was easier and more economical to build data centres near power sources than near coastal cable landing hubs such as Chennai.\n\"It will be much more economical if you go closer to where the power itself is generating,\" he said, adding that the government would continue to facilitate faster approvals to meet the requirements of both the country and the world.\nMicrosoft on Monday formally launched its fourth India cloud region, in Hyderabad, as part of its USD 20.5 billion investment commitment to the country's digital and AI infrastructure.\nThe India South Central region, which became generally available on August 6, joins the company's",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-21T10:36:52"
    },
    {
      "title": "Microsoft launches 4th India cloud region in Hyderabad to boost AI capacity",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/news/microsoft-launches-4th-india-cloud-region-in-hyderabad-to-boost-ai-capacity-126092100431_1.html",
      "description": "Microsoft on Monday formally launched its fourth India cloud region, in Hyderabad, as part of its USD 20.5 billion investment commitment to the country's digital and AI infrastructure.\nThe India South Central region, which became generally available on August 6, joins the company's existing regions in Pune, Chennai and Mumbai.\nThe Hyderabad facility uses effectively zero water for cooling, relying on air-cooled chillers instead of traditional water-based systems, Microsoft India and South Asia President Puneet Chandok said.\nHe noted that the country has about 2 GW of data centre capacity, which is set to rise to 6-7 GW by 2035, with over USD 100 billion in investments already committed.\nIndia would emerge as an AI infrastructure hub not just for itself but for Asia and the Global South, Chandok said.\nIn a video message, Microsoft Vice Chair and President Brad Smith said the new region would give organisations in India, and more broadly Asia, more capacity, greater resilience and m",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-21T08:00:29"
    }
  ]
